From b17056f2cac4a038c22cbc66cd332f76a621e3be Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ChenXiaoSong <chenxiaosong@kylinos.cn>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 16:05:22 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 4/7] smb/server: expose native symbolic links as reparse
 points

Existing symlinks are visible in directory listings but opening them
returns STATUS_ACCESS_DENIED. Expose their targets with
IO_REPARSE_TAG_SYMLINK and FSCTL_GET_REPARSE_POINT. Use O_PATH for opens
with FILE_OPEN_REPARSE_POINT, and return STATUS_STOPPED_ON_SYMLINK for
ordinary opens so the client resolves the target.

Locate the first link without following server-side symlinks. Encode
the remaining path length in UTF-16 bytes, reserve response space, and
handle SMB3.1.1 error contexts and related compound errors together.

Example (export a fresh /export as test, with POSIX extensions enabled):

  1. Server:
     printf 'target\n' > /export/file
     mkdir /export/subdir
     printf 'nested\n' > /export/subdir/file
     ln -s file /export/symlink
     ln -s subdir /export/dirlink
     ln -s missing /export/dangling
  2. Client:
     for mode in noposix posix; do
         mount -t cifs //${server_ip}/test /mnt \
             -o username=${smb_user},vers=3.1.1,$mode || exit 1
         ls -l /mnt/symlink /mnt/dirlink /mnt/dangling
         readlink /mnt/symlink
         cat /mnt/symlink
         cat /mnt/dirlink/file
         readlink /mnt/dangling
         umount /mnt || exit 1
     done

Both mounts show links. The four commands after ls print file, target,
nested and missing respectively. Opening the missing target still fails.
Remote ln -s is not enabled yet. Non-POSIX absolute targets are outside
the supported scope; POSIX absolute targets refer to the client's root.

Code and test instructions generated with OpenAI Codex assistance.

Fixes: 4ea477988c42 ("ksmbd: remove follow symlinks support")
